//? Title: Cantor–Zassenhaus: Actual Degree Buckets, Random Splits and Exact Checks
//? Subtitle: Prime-field native arithmetic, irreducibility checks and character powers beyond uint64
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Factoring a polynomial over a prime field asks for irreducible factors whose
product recovers its coefficients, including any leading unit. Reconstruction
alone cannot establish irreducibility. We retain the original quartic over GF(5)
and its factor-degree plot, inspect actual distinct-degree and randomized
same-degree splits, and verify both reconstruction and irreducibility. Reusable
arithmetic and formatting now live in datamunge. The checked implementation
rejects invalid fields and repeated factors, preserves the legacy seeded stream,
and avoids a wrapped p-to-the-d exponent. Native traces expose actual work and
complete-call caps. This is a squarefree odd-prime-field implementation, without
squarefree decomposition or a characteristic-two randomized splitter.
)

\toc

> The original quartic: one actual degree test and a residual shortcut

Over GF(5), use f=(x-1)(x-2)(x*x+2). The coefficient vector is
[4,4,4,2,1], in increasing power order. The quadratic is irreducible: squares
in GF(5) are 0,1,4, while x*x=-2 would require the nonsquare 3.
The native trace performs one distinct-degree test at d=1. Its gcd collects
both linear factors into x*x+2*x+2. Dividing leaves x*x+2; the remaining
polynomial is already too small to contain two factors of the next possible
degree, so it is emitted directly. There is no executed d=2 gcd in this example.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cantor_zassenhaus_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto linears=dm::algorithms::poly_multiply_gf({4,1},{3,1},5);
  const auto f=dm::algorithms::poly_multiply_gf(linears,{2,0,1},5);
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::cantor_zassenhaus_trace(f,5);
  if(f!=dm::algorithms::GFPoly{4,4,4,2,1} || trace.distinct_degree.size()!=1 || trace.factors.size()!=3) return 1;
  const auto& step=trace.distinct_degree[0];
  std::print("Input: {}.\n\n| tested degree | remaining before | actual Frobenius remainder | actual gcd bucket | remaining after |\n"
             "| ---: | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n",dm::algorithms::poly_string_gf(f,5));
  std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",step.degree,dm::algorithms::poly_string_gf(step.remaining_before,5),
      dm::algorithms::poly_string_gf(step.frobenius,5),dm::algorithms::poly_string_gf(step.bucket,5),
      dm::algorithms::poly_string_gf(step.remaining_after,5));
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(65,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle node;node.width=6.4;node.height=1.8;
  diagram.node("input",{0,0},std::format("monic f over GF(5)\n{}",dm::algorithms::poly_string_gf(trace.monic_input,5)),node);
  node.width=4.8;node.height=1.8;
  diagram.node("bucket",{-5,-3.5},std::format("actual d={} gcd bucket\n{}",step.degree,dm::algorithms::poly_string_gf(step.bucket,5)),node);
  node.fill={220,252,231};
  diagram.node("residual",{5,-3.5},std::format("residual shortcut\n{}",dm::algorithms::poly_string_gf(trace.residual,5)),node)
      .edge("input","bucket","Frobenius, gcd").edge("input","residual","exact quotient");
  node.width=2.8;node.height=1.3;
  diagram.node("left",{-7.5,-6.5},dm::algorithms::poly_string_gf(trace.factors[0],5),node)
      .node("right",{-2.5,-6.5},dm::algorithms::poly_string_gf(trace.factors[1],5),node)
      .edge("bucket","left","EDF split").edge("bucket","right","complement")
      .text({0,-8},"actual factors: two linears and one irreducible quadratic",19)
      .save_svg("build/cantor_zassenhaus_method.svg");
  std::print("\nActual DDF steps: {}; random attempts: {}; modular polynomial products: {}; gcd requests: {}.\n",
      trace.work.distinct_degree_steps,trace.work.random_trials,trace.work.modular_products,trace.work.gcd_calls);
}
```
