| tested degree | remaining before | actual Frobenius remainder | actual gcd bucket | remaining after |
| ---: | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| 1 | x^4 + 2x^3 + 4x^2 + 4x + 4 | 4x^2 + 4x + 3 | x^2 + 2x + 2 | x^2 + 2 |

Actual DDF steps: 1; random attempts: 6; modular polynomial products: 10; gcd requests: 8.
\image(figures/cantor_zassenhaus_method.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The original quartic's executed path. The linear bucket is split by EDF; the quadratic is the actual residual shortcut.)
\alttext(A quartic branches into a degree-one bucket containing two linear factors and a residual irreducible quadratic. The bucket then branches into its two actual linear factors.)

Why does the degree test work? Over GF(p), x to the p-to-the-d minus x is the
product of all monic irreducibles whose degrees divide d, without repeats. Once
smaller degrees have been removed, the next gcd therefore extracts only degree-d
factors. The implementation carries one Frobenius iterate forward, reducing it
when the remaining modulus shrinks:

$$
h_d=x^{p^d}\bmod f_{\rm remaining},\qquad
 g_d=\gcd(f_{\rm remaining},h_d-x).
$$
\alttext(The Frobenius iterate is x raised to p raised to d modulo the remaining polynomial. Its difference from x has a gcd with that remainder that extracts the next degree bucket.)

If the remaining degree becomes less than 2*d while every remaining irreducible
has degree at least d, there cannot be two remaining factors. This proves the
residual shortcut. These statements concern polynomial arithmetic, not a
promise that forming or manipulating large powers is free. The native method
uses modular powering throughout. See [Shoup, Chapter 20](https://shoup.net/ntb/ntb-v2.pdf).

> Preserve the degree bars, and verify more than reconstruction

The original bars have heights 1,1,2. We regenerate them from the actual output
order, which can vary with the seed, and call the native irreducibility test on
every returned factor. Native multiplication replaces the report-local product
helper; native formatting replaces its polynomial-to-string implementation.
As a control, the single-element list [f] reconstructs f immediately, although
its only member is reducible. Multiplication alone would accept that false
factorization. The separate irreducibility check is essential.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cantor_zassenhaus.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const dm::algorithms::GFPoly f{4,4,4,2,1};
  const auto factors=dm::algorithms::cantor_zassenhaus(f,5);
  dm::algorithms::GFPoly product{1};std::vector<double> degrees;std::vector<std::string> labels;
  std::print("| factor | normalized coefficients | degree | independently called irreducibility test |\n"
             "| :--- | :--- | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<factors.size();++i) {
    const auto& factor=factors[i];const bool irreducible=dm::algorithms::poly_is_irreducible_gf(factor,5);
    if(!irreducible) return 1;
    product=dm::algorithms::poly_multiply_gf(product,factor,5);
    degrees.push_back(factor.size()-1);labels.push_back(std::format("f{}",i+1));
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",dm::algorithms::poly_string_gf(factor,5),factor,factor.size()-1,irreducible);
  }
  if(product!=f) return 1;
  std::print("\nProduct: {}. Reconstructs f: {}.\n",dm::algorithms::poly_string_gf(product,5),product==f);
  std::print("Single candidate [f]: reconstructs {}, but irreducible {}.\n",dm::algorithms::poly_multiply_gf({1},f,5)==f,
      dm::algorithms::poly_is_irreducible_gf(f,5));
  dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(degrees,labels,"factor degree",dm::RGB{124,58,237})
      .title("Original GF(5) quartic: factor degrees 1, 1, 2")
      .x_label("actual output factor").y_label("degree").y_limits(0,3)
      .save_svg("build/cantor_zassenhaus.svg");
}
```
