\caption(The retained factor-degree plot comes from actual returned factors, each checked separately for irreducibility.)
\alttext(Three bars have heights one, one and two, corresponding to two linear factors and one irreducible quadratic.)

The irreducibility API uses repeated Frobenius gcds to exclude every possible
irreducible degree up to half the input degree. A reducible polynomial must have
such a factor. For the native tests, an additional, separate monic trial-division
oracle establishes factor multisets on small fields, so validation does not rely
only on calling another production function that shares arithmetic kernels.

> Same-degree splitting: inspect attempts, then vary the seed

The quadratic bucket (x*x+1)(x*x+x+2) over GF(3) contains two irreducibles
of degree d=2. Random h may share a proper gcd with the bucket, which gives an
immediate split. Otherwise the algorithm computes its quadratic character in
each extension field and takes another gcd:

$$
v=h^{(p^d-1)/2}\bmod g,\qquad a=\gcd(v-1,g).
$$
\alttext(Raise the sampled polynomial h to one half of p to the d minus one modulo the bucket g. The gcd of v minus one with g proposes a factor.)

In an ideal uniform sample conditioned on nonzero residues in all k component
fields, the characters are independent signs. Only the all-positive and
all-negative patterns fail to separate them, so this model's nontrivial-split
probability is \(1-2^{1-k}\). The implementation retains the existing
modulo-reduced xorshift64 stream and rejects constant candidates. Its samples
are deterministic and slightly biased. The ideal model does not prove a
termination or expected-time bound for every fixed seed of this implementation.

The table records one actual two-quadratic run. The plot then varies 64 seeds
for the product of all three monic irreducible quadratics over GF(3). At least
two accepted binary splits are needed, while retries and discarded constant
candidates add work. Trial counts, raw-word counts and modular products measure
different operations; none is a wall-clock timing.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cantor_zassenhaus_trials.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto pair=dm::algorithms::poly_multiply_gf({1,0,1},{2,1,1},3);
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::cantor_zassenhaus_trace(pair,3);
  std::print("| attempt | target degree | sampled coefficients | initial gcd | used character | character coefficients | proposed gcd | accepted |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n");
  for(const auto& trial:trace.equal_degree)
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",trial.attempt,trial.target.size()-1,trial.candidate,
        trial.initial_gcd,trial.used_character,trial.character,trial.split_gcd,trial.accepted);
  const auto three=dm::algorithms::poly_multiply_gf(pair,{2,2,1},3);
  std::vector<double> seeds,trials;std::size_t minimum=std::numeric_limits<std::size_t>::max(),maximum=0;
  std::print("\n| seed | random trials | raw words | modular products | gcd requests | factors |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::uint64_t seed=0;seed<64;++seed) {
    dm::algorithms::CantorZassenhausOptions options;options.seed=seed;options.max_random_trials=1000;
    const auto run=dm::algorithms::cantor_zassenhaus_trace(three,3,options);
    dm::algorithms::GFPoly product{1};for(const auto& factor:run.factors) {
      if(!dm::algorithms::poly_is_irreducible_gf(factor,3) || factor.size()!=3) return 1;
      product=dm::algorithms::poly_multiply_gf(product,factor,3);
    }
    if(product!=three || run.factors.size()!=3 || run.work.random_trials<2) return 1;
    seeds.push_back(seed);trials.push_back(run.work.random_trials);
    minimum=std::min(minimum,run.work.random_trials);maximum=std::max(maximum,run.work.random_trials);
    if(seed<8) std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",seed,run.work.random_trials,run.work.random_words,
        run.work.modular_products,run.work.gcd_calls,run.factors.size());
  }
  std::print("\nAll 64 seeds reconstruct three irreducible quadratics; random-trial range {} to {}.\n",minimum,maximum);
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create().line(seeds,trials,"random trials",{37,99,235},1.8)
      .points(seeds,trials,"",{37,99,235},3).line({0,63},{2,2},"two accepted splits",{148,163,184},1.5)
      .title("Three GF(3) quadratics: actual trial count by seed")
      .x_label("legacy xorshift seed (zero uses its documented fallback)").y_label("whole-call random trials")
      .y_limits(0,static_cast<double>(maximum)+4).save_svg("build/cantor_zassenhaus_trials.svg");
}
```
