> Avoid the wrapped exponent: a near-INT64-limit field

The old code materialized p^d in uint64 before forming (p^d-1)/2. That
wraps even for modest d. Instead set c=h^((p-1)/2) modulo g and use

$$
e_1=(p-1)/2,\qquad e_{j+1}=p e_j+(p-1)/2,\qquad
v_{j+1}=v_j^p c\bmod g.
$$
\alttext(Start the exponent at one half of p minus one. Multiply the previous exponent by p and add that same half. The corresponding polynomial value is raised to p and multiplied by c modulo g.)

Induction gives e_j=(p^j-1)/2. Only scalar exponents p and (p-1)/2 are
passed to modular powering, so p^d is never stored. This algebraic recurrence
works in the quotient ring even if h has zero residues; the initial gcd route
already handles proper shared factors.

Take p=9,223,372,036,854,775,783, an odd prime near the signed-64-bit limit.
Since p is 7 modulo 8, both -1 and -2 are nonsquares, so x*x+1 and x*x+2
are irreducible quadratics. Their product requires d=2 character splitting,
where p*p is far beyond uint64. The program factors it with bounded work and
checks both factors and reconstruction. Coefficient sums/products are also
widened before reduction, fixing separate signed-overflow paths.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  constexpr std::int64_t p=9223372036854775783LL;
  const dm::algorithms::GFPoly first{1,0,1},second{2,0,1};
  const auto f=dm::algorithms::poly_multiply_gf(first,second,p);
  dm::algorithms::CantorZassenhausOptions options;options.max_random_trials=100;options.max_modular_products=100000;
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::cantor_zassenhaus_trace(f,p,options);
  dm::algorithms::GFPoly product{1};
  std::print("Prime p: {}. Input coefficients: {}.\n",p,f);
  for(const auto& factor:trace.factors) {
    const bool irreducible=dm::algorithms::poly_is_irreducible_gf(factor,p);
    if(!irreducible || factor.size()!=3) return 1;
    std::print("Factor {}: irreducible {}.\n",dm::algorithms::poly_string_gf(factor,p),irreducible);
    product=dm::algorithms::poly_multiply_gf(product,factor,p);
  }
  if(product!=f || trace.factors.size()!=2 || trace.distinct_degree.size()!=2) return 1;
  std::print("Reconstructed: {}. Actual DDF steps: {}; random trials: {}; modular power calls: {}; modular products: {}.\n",
      product==f,trace.work.distinct_degree_steps,trace.work.random_trials,trace.work.modular_power_calls,trace.work.modular_products);
  std::print("Large-residue product: {}.\n",dm::algorithms::poly_multiply_gf({p-1,p-1},{1,1},p));
  auto exact=options;exact.max_modular_products=trace.work.modular_products;
  const auto bounded=dm::algorithms::cantor_zassenhaus_with_options(f,p,exact);
  bool rejected=false;--exact.max_modular_products;
  try {(void)dm::algorithms::cantor_zassenhaus_with_options(f,p,exact);} catch(const std::length_error&) {rejected=true;}
  if(bounded!=trace.factors || !rejected) return 1;
  std::print("Exact product budget succeeds; one-short budget rejected: {}.\n",rejected);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Prime p: 9223372036854775783. Input coefficients: [2, 0, 3, 0, 1].
Factor x^2 + 2: irreducible true.
Factor x^2 + 1: irreducible true.
Reconstructed: true. Actual DDF steps: 2; random trials: 2; modular power calls: 6; modular products: 736.
Large-residue product: [9223372036854775782, 9223372036854775781, 9223372036854775782].
Exact product budget succeeds; one-short budget rejected: true.
// result_end

The native test independently evaluates every recorded character in this case
with an unsigned-128-bit exponent and separate modular polynomial arithmetic.
Thus the large-exponent check is not merely a product reconstruction test.
The public implementation itself does not need a multiword exponent container.

> Units, repeated factors and complete-call caps

The original API normalizes a nonmonic input to its monic associate. Its factor
product therefore loses the leading unit unless the caller restores it. The
trace retains that unit. Nonzero constants return an empty factor list; zero
has no such factorization and is rejected. Repeated-factor inputs are rejected
using gcd(f,f'), including nonconstant polynomials with zero derivative in
positive characteristic. A full factorizer would first perform squarefree
decomposition and track multiplicities; this API does not. Characteristic-two
arithmetic/irreducibility helpers work, but the odd-field randomized splitter
rejects p=2. The [FLINT documentation](https://flintlib.org/doc/fq_poly_factor.html)
describes a fuller finite-field factorization interface with those extra stages.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto nonmonic=dm::algorithms::cantor_zassenhaus_trace({3,0,2},5);
  dm::algorithms::GFPoly product{1};for(const auto& factor:nonmonic.factors) product=dm::algorithms::poly_multiply_gf(product,factor,5);
  const auto restored=dm::algorithms::poly_multiply_gf(product,{nonmonic.leading_coefficient},5);
  if(product!=nonmonic.monic_input || restored!=nonmonic.input) return 1;
  std::print("Nonmonic input {}, unit {}, monic factor product {}, restored {}.\n",nonmonic.input,nonmonic.leading_coefficient,product,restored);
  bool repeated=false;try {(void)dm::algorithms::cantor_zassenhaus({1,2,1},5);} catch(const std::invalid_argument&) {repeated=true;}
  bool zero_derivative=false;try {(void)dm::algorithms::cantor_zassenhaus({1,0,0,1},3);} catch(const std::invalid_argument&) {zero_derivative=true;}
  bool zero_divisor=false;try {(void)dm::algorithms::poly_divmod_gf({1},{0,5},5);} catch(const std::invalid_argument&) {zero_divisor=true;}
  bool even_field=false;try {(void)dm::algorithms::cantor_zassenhaus({1,1},2);} catch(const std::invalid_argument&) {even_field=true;}
  dm::algorithms::GFPoly six{1};for(std::int64_t root=0;root<6;++root) six=dm::algorithms::poly_multiply_gf(six,{-root,1},7);
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::cantor_zassenhaus_trace(six,7);
  dm::algorithms::CantorZassenhausOptions options;options.max_random_trials=trace.work.random_trials-1;
  bool trial_cap=false;try {(void)dm::algorithms::cantor_zassenhaus_with_options(six,7,options);} catch(const std::length_error&) {trial_cap=true;}
  if(!repeated || !zero_derivative || !zero_divisor || !even_field || !trial_cap) return 1;
  std::print("Rejected repeated input {}, zero-derivative repeat {}, reduced-zero divisor {}, even factorization field {}.\n",
      repeated,zero_derivative,zero_divisor,even_field);
  std::print("Six distinct linear factors: {} random trials; one-short whole-call trial budget rejected {}.\n",trace.work.random_trials,trial_cap);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Nonmonic input [3, 0, 2], unit 2, monic factor product [4, 0, 1], restored [3, 0, 2].
Rejected repeated input true, zero-derivative repeat true, reduced-zero divisor true, even factorization field true.
Six distinct linear factors: 6 random trials; one-short whole-call trial budget rejected true.
// result_end

Both budgets are complete-call caps, checked before the excess random draw or
modular polynomial product. They return no partial factor list. Zero means
unlimited. The counts exclude scalar primality validation, coefficient operations,
gcd/division work and wall time. Ordinary and traced APIs use the same kernel;
ordinary calls keep no owned DDF/EDF records. Source coefficients are unchanged.

With schoolbook dense arithmetic, modular products and divisions have quadratic
field work in degree. Incremental DDF has an O(n^3*log(p)) upper bound in this
field-operation model. A character trial on a degree-m bucket with common
factor degree d costs O(d*m*m*log(p)) such work, plus scalar inversions and
sampling. No fast multiplication, modular composition or extension-field
representation is supplied. An untraced run uses linear coefficient scratch
storage; a trace retains the sum of all recorded polynomial lengths and can
be much larger. No natural observational dataset is needed for these exact
algebraic properties; controlled field/polynomial inputs expose them directly.

Native verification enumerates 7,800 monic inputs over GF(3), GF(5) and GF(7),
with a separate monic trial-division oracle. All 6,258 squarefree cases factor
correctly and all 1,542 repeated cases reject. A further 1,000 random input draws supply squarefree cases for seeded checks;
300 near-limit arithmetic cases, original division/root regressions, exact
budgets, units, invalid fields/divisors and owned copies are checked. Actual
Frobenius values, candidate gcds, character values, accepted complements and
work-counter formulas are verified independently. Reconstruction and
irreducibility are separate requirements throughout.

> References

- D. G. Cantor and H. Zassenhaus (1981). [A New Algorithm for Factoring Polynomials Over Finite Fields](https://doi.org/10.1090/S0025-5718-1981-0606517-5). Mathematics of Computation 36(154), 587-592.
- V. Shoup. [A Computational Introduction to Number Theory and Algebra, version 2](https://shoup.net/ntb/ntb-v2.pdf), Chapters 17 and 20. Prime-field arithmetic, irreducibility and degree splitting.
- FLINT. [Finite-field polynomial factorization interface](https://flintlib.org/doc/fq_poly_factor.html). Complete factorization includes squarefree handling beyond this restricted API.
